# SYNOPSIS

```c
vx_status vx_handle_dup(vx_handle h, uint32_t rights, vx_handle *out);
vx_status vx_handle_close(vx_handle h);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A task names each kernel object it may use by a handle: a 32-bit value that is an index into the task's handle table in its low 16 bits and that slot's generation in its high 16 (see the kernel's design, 01 §3).
The generation is bumped each time the slot is freed, so a handle kept after it was closed fails with `VX_ERR_BAD_HANDLE` instead of reaching whatever took the slot next.
No valid handle is 0, which is `VX_HANDLE_NONE`.
Slots are taken lowest first, so a program run twice sees the same handle values.
A table holds 255 handles.

Each handle carries rights, the bits named in `abi/vx/rights.def`: `VX_RIGHT_READ`, `WRITE`, `EXEC`, `MAP`, `DUPLICATE`, `TRANSFER`, `SIGNAL`, `WAIT`, `MANAGE`, `INSPECT`, `DEBUG` and `PAGER`.
Every call checks that its handles carry the rights it needs, and refuses with `VX_ERR_ACCESS` if they do not; each call's page says which.

`vx_handle_dup` makes a second handle, in the caller's table, to the object <h> names, with <rights>, and writes it to `*`<out>.
<rights> may only be the same as <h>'s or fewer, never more; `VX_RIGHTS_SAME` gives the new handle exactly <h>'s rights.
<h> needs `VX_RIGHT_DUPLICATE`.
A handle given away with fewer rights is how a task shares an object it must not let others change: a VMO to read but not write, a task to inspect but not kill.

`vx_handle_close` removes <h> from the caller's table and drops its reference to the object.
It needs no rights.
The object lives on while anything else holds it: another handle, a mapping of a VMO (see as(2)), a message in flight.
When the last goes the object is destroyed, and what that means is the object's: closing a channel's or ring's end tells its peer, which sees `VX_ERR_PEER_CLOSED`.

Handles move between tasks only in channel and ring messages, and only with `VX_RIGHT_TRANSFER`; the write moves them out of the writer's table.

`handle_replace` is reserved.
The design names it among the handle calls without describing it (01 §3): it is meant to swap a handle for a new one with the same or fewer rights in one step, the old one closed.
It has no vx-rt wrapper, and `vx_syscall(VX_SYS_handle_replace, ...)` returns `VX_ERR_UNSUPPORTED` today.

# EXAMPLES

A handle duplicated with fewer rights, never more, from the kernel's tests (`tests/kernel/ktest.c`):

```c
vx_handle weak;
CHECK(vx_handle_dup(got, VX_RIGHT_READ, &weak) == VX_OK);
CHECK(vx_counter_read(weak) == 5);
CHECK(vx_counter_signal(weak, 9) == VX_ERR_ACCESS);
vx_handle strong;
CHECK(vx_handle_dup(weak, VX_RIGHT_READ | VX_RIGHT_SIGNAL, &strong) ==
      VX_ERR_ACCESS);
vx_handle_close(weak);
```

